WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en-US

00:00:00.320 --> 00:00:05.840
Yuriko the Tiger’s Shadow is a pretty unique card.&nbsp;
It’s the most popular Dimir commander by a country&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:05.840 --> 00:00:11.440
mile. It’s one of only two creature-type-based&nbsp;
commanders that sees common cEDH play, the other&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:11.440 --> 00:00:17.680
being Magda of course. And, Yuriko does not pay&nbsp;
commander tax. I guess if you’re casting her for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:17.680 --> 00:00:23.040
her base cost she does, but more realistically&nbsp;
Yuriko almost always gets cast for a Commander&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:23.040 --> 00:00:29.120
Ninjutsu cost, which is forever pegged at 2&nbsp;
mana. Last week I talked about Eminence as one&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:29.120 --> 00:00:33.920
of the two major exceptions to the way commanders&nbsp;
work, not even requiring a commander to be on the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:33.920 --> 00:00:40.240
board to have an initial effect, and this is the&nbsp;
other—not costing additional mana for subsequent&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:40.240 --> 00:00:45.680
commander casts. Yuriko in particular has been&nbsp;
singled out as a design mistake, including by&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:45.680 --> 00:00:51.280
Gavin Verhey who worked on the original card,&nbsp;
and I agree with that. Commander-ninjutsu is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:51.280 --> 00:00:56.320
this bespoke ability created with the one&nbsp;
task of bringing ninjutsu into the command&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:56.320 --> 00:01:00.160
zone on a single, and the fact that command&nbsp;
tax wasn’t a part of that ability seems like&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:00.160 --> 00:01:04.960
an obvious oversight in retrospect. The results&nbsp;
of this have bled into the player experience,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:04.960 --> 00:01:09.920
with Yuriko commanding a similar salt score to&nbsp;
that of Edgar Markov, the most contentious of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:09.920 --> 00:01:15.360
the Eminence commanders. People are bothered by&nbsp;
this card, and they dread playing against it.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:15.360 --> 00:01:20.880
However, I think it’s interesting to dig into why&nbsp;
Yuriko is a problem for people, and how important&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:20.880 --> 00:01:26.480
her tax evasion is in that equation. Because&nbsp;
I think the line for not paying command tax&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:26.480 --> 00:01:32.000
is actually blurrier than it seems, and more&nbsp;
sensitive to the specifics of a given card,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:32.000 --> 00:01:36.720
with Yuriko’s other features being equally&nbsp;
important as her unique method of entry in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:36.720 --> 00:01:42.320
creating her infamous status. Broadly, there are&nbsp;
two things that contribute to Yuriko in particular&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:42.320 --> 00:01:48.720
having a uniquely frustrating play pattern for&nbsp;
a lot of players—her vibes, and her power level.

00:01:48.720 --> 00:01:54.400
By vibes I mean that the way Yuriko cheats&nbsp;
commander tax feels uniquely bad among commanders,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:54.400 --> 00:01:59.360
and to demonstrate that we should talk about some&nbsp;
other tax evaders, and why they don’t drum up the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:59.360 --> 00:02:04.640
same sort of ire. Firstly, let’s talk about&nbsp;
the Squees of the world, legendary creatures&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:04.640 --> 00:02:09.440
that can be cast from graveyard. On the surface,&nbsp;
these may seem similar to something like Yuriko&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:09.440 --> 00:02:14.560
since you can continue to cast them for some&nbsp;
fixed cost after they die, but the comparison&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:14.560 --> 00:02:19.520
quickly falls apart. Firstly, all but one of&nbsp;
them will be whisked back to the command zone by&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:19.520 --> 00:02:26.240
any now-commonplace exile-based removal, but more&nbsp;
importantly, they obey the rules of 60 card magic.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:26.240 --> 00:02:31.440
These cards are designed with the idea of them&nbsp;
being cast over and over baked into the design,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:31.440 --> 00:02:36.160
and their function in a 60 card format will&nbsp;
be identical to their function in commander&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:36.160 --> 00:02:40.720
after the first cast. The uniquely potent&nbsp;
thing about Commander is the command zone,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:40.720 --> 00:02:45.680
and these are skipping out on a large portion&nbsp;
of the benefit of that zone. One step up from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:45.680 --> 00:02:52.640
these would be Hogaak, a commander with the two&nbsp;
different discount keywords of convoke and delve.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:52.640 --> 00:02:58.960
Hogaak also shares being castable from the&nbsp;
graveyard, but perhaps more relevantly, regardless&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:58.960 --> 00:03:04.240
of where the card is being cast from you won’t be&nbsp;
spending mana. If it gets exiled a couple times,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:04.240 --> 00:03:09.520
the command zone cost will go up accordingly, but&nbsp;
paying in exiles from graveyard or tapped creature&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:09.520 --> 00:03:14.960
tokens will often feel much less meaningful than&nbsp;
paying in mana—you won’t experience paying for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:14.960 --> 00:03:20.640
Hogaak as a linear barrier in the same way you&nbsp;
would with most other commanders. Nonetheless,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:20.640 --> 00:03:26.880
Hogaak feels fine because itspayment method still&nbsp;
performs the gesture of paying command tax. A big&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:26.880 --> 00:03:32.000
part of what makes Yuriko frustrating is that it&nbsp;
feels like the Yuriko player is exempted from a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:32.000 --> 00:03:36.640
core rule of the commander format—when other&nbsp;
commanders get removed, they’re harder to cast,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:36.640 --> 00:03:41.600
but Yuriko simply isn’t. Even if Hogaak&nbsp;
bucks some of the conventional wisdom,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:41.600 --> 00:03:46.720
it doesn’t feel like a major outlier because&nbsp;
you’re still expending additional resources.

00:03:46.720 --> 00:03:52.000
This idea comes into even sharper focus when&nbsp;
we consider Liesa. Liesa, Shroud of Dusk is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:52.000 --> 00:03:56.640
a 5 mana commander who, instead of requiring&nbsp;
2 more mana for each subsequent command zone&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:56.640 --> 00:04:02.080
cast, instead requires paying 2 life for each&nbsp;
previous cast. As a Liesa player I will tell&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:02.080 --> 00:04:06.880
you right now, that is not a meaningful&nbsp;
cost. In the 40 life format of commander,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:06.880 --> 00:04:11.120
most players are plenty willing to fork&nbsp;
over 3 life in exchange for one mana,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:11.120 --> 00:04:16.640
and 2 life for one mana, as seen in something like&nbsp;
a Phyrexian mana symbol or an untapped shockland,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:16.640 --> 00:04:22.560
is pretty much a no brainer. Cutting that&nbsp;
no-brainer rate in half, 1 life for 1 mana,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:22.560 --> 00:04:28.720
makes it basically free and will rarely feel even&nbsp;
a little bit relevant. So, why doesn’t Liesa come&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:28.720 --> 00:04:35.040
to mind as a chronic tax evader? The answer, along&nbsp;
with other factors we’ll get to later, is The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:35.040 --> 00:04:41.040
Gesture. If my Liesa dies, when I cast her again,&nbsp;
I adjust my life total die down two numbers. If I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:41.040 --> 00:04:46.800
need to cast her again after that, four numbers.&nbsp;
It’s not significant, but it’s something,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:46.800 --> 00:04:51.680
an acknowledgement that my commander has died and&nbsp;
this is not the first time I’m casting her. I’m&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:51.680 --> 00:04:56.160
not playing by the exact same rules as everybody&nbsp;
else, but I’m still playing the same format,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:56.160 --> 00:05:01.440
and I recognize the existence of that format’s&nbsp;
rules. This brings me to an example that, while&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:01.440 --> 00:05:07.920
less problematic than Yuriko, is a bit hairier&nbsp;
than the ones I’ve mentioned so far—Derevi.

00:05:07.920 --> 00:05:12.560
She is a 3 mana bant commander with the ability&nbsp;
to pay 4 mana to put her from the command zone&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:12.560 --> 00:05:18.320
into play at instant speed. This ability doesn’t&nbsp;
perform the gesture, and it does kinda totally&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:18.320 --> 00:05:24.880
cheat command tax. So… what makes this different&nbsp;
from Yuriko? This is where the details get more&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:24.880 --> 00:05:28.640
granular from a purely costs perspective. You&nbsp;
could argue that 4 mana is larger than 3 mana,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:28.640 --> 00:05:33.280
so Derevi’s cost will usually go up at least&nbsp;
once, though it’s not hard to imagine somebody&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:33.280 --> 00:05:38.320
arguing that flash and uncounterability mitigates&nbsp;
this difference, making the 4 mana cast option an&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:38.320 --> 00:05:44.240
appealing one in its own right. To me, the&nbsp;
more potent element is the raw number—4 is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:44.240 --> 00:05:50.240
a lot more than 2. Paying 2 mana for a commander&nbsp;
repeatedly is quite inherently powerful. You hit&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:50.240 --> 00:05:54.560
the field early, and then subsequent plays will&nbsp;
leave boatloads of open mana for follow-up plays&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:54.560 --> 00:05:58.640
or counterspells or so on. By contrast, paying 4&nbsp;
mana repeatedly for Derevi or 5 mana repeatedly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:58.640 --> 00:06:06.560
for Liesa feels more meaningful as a cost. It’s&nbsp;
still quite nice to have spare mana later in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:06.560 --> 00:06:11.520
game when you’re on 7 or 8 lands and not needing&nbsp;
to worry about having an uncastably expensive&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:11.520 --> 00:06:18.160
commander, but that dynamic exists on a decidedly&nbsp;
lesser caliber than something like Yuriko.

00:06:18.160 --> 00:06:21.920
So far I’ve been ignoring the text boxes&nbsp;
of all the cards I’ve been talking about,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:21.920 --> 00:06:26.240
which has its upsides in isolating&nbsp;
specific design elements. However,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:26.240 --> 00:06:31.920
now it’s time to talk about the other reason&nbsp;
Yuriko feels so frustrating—the power level.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:31.920 --> 00:06:36.480
What I mean by power level here is simply how much&nbsp;
power you get based on the amount of mana you pay.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:36.480 --> 00:06:41.760
Yuriko’s text box does such a large amount for&nbsp;
2 mana that it’s almost absurd. The actual text&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:41.760 --> 00:06:46.320
reads “Whenever a ninja you control deals combat&nbsp;
damage to a player, reveal the top card of your&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:46.320 --> 00:06:51.280
library and put it into your hand. Each opponent&nbsp;
loses life equal to that card’s mana value.”&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:51.280 --> 00:06:55.440
If we wanted to grossly simplify this,&nbsp;
we could replace this with the text:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:55.440 --> 00:06:59.760
whenever a ninja you control deals combat damage&nbsp;
to a player, draw a card and each opponent loses&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:59.760 --> 00:07:05.120
2 life. Trying to find an equivalent card led to&nbsp;
Seshiro the Anointed, a 6 mana snakes commander&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:05.120 --> 00:07:10.160
who could realistically be more like 4 mana&nbsp;
given the niche position of snakes, and Tovolar,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:10.160 --> 00:07:14.880
a 3 mana wolf and werewolf commander with a bit&nbsp;
of added utility for flipping werewolves but no&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:14.880 --> 00:07:20.240
equivalent damage ability. And Tovolar is a pretty&nbsp;
good typal commander, a whole lot of tribes would&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:20.240 --> 00:07:24.640
swoon at receiving that level of utility from&nbsp;
the command zone. So we’ll say that Yuriko is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:24.640 --> 00:07:29.520
along the lines of a powerful 3 mana commander or&nbsp;
a moderately-powered 4 mana commander, and it’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:29.520 --> 00:07:35.920
castable for 2 mana as many times as you like as&nbsp;
long as you’re able to pay the ninjutsu cost. So,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:35.920 --> 00:07:42.800
how hard is that? Well, a quick visit to the&nbsp;
Yuriko EDHREC page makes that clear. Ornithopter&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:42.800 --> 00:07:48.080
exists as an evasive 0-cost creature that can&nbsp;
get Yuriko into play, and there are a ton of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:48.080 --> 00:07:53.120
great evasive 1 mana creatures that can do the&nbsp;
same. Thousand-faced Shadow, Changeling Outcast,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:53.120 --> 00:07:58.000
and Mothdust Changeling are all also Ninjas,&nbsp;
and there are other cards like Faerie Seer,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:58.000 --> 00:08:02.080
Siren Stormtamer, and Hope of Ghirapur that&nbsp;
have meaningful other upside with Yuriko’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:02.080 --> 00:08:09.520
gameplan. It decidedly is a cost, but given&nbsp;
that other Ninjutsu creatures can make use&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:09.520 --> 00:08:14.880
of these evasive little doofuses as well,&nbsp;
it’s hard to call it too dramatic a cost.

00:08:14.880 --> 00:08:19.840
By comparison, Derevi’s untapping&nbsp;
effect for 3ish mana is far from bad,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:19.840 --> 00:08:26.000
but it doesn’t give direct value or direct damage,&nbsp;
unlike Yuriko which gives both for 2-3 mana.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:26.000 --> 00:08:31.280
Derevi is a good commander and you can do a lot of&nbsp;
things with her, but they require the convergence&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:31.280 --> 00:08:36.960
of other elements of your deck, and it’s perfectly&nbsp;
possible to stall out even if Derevi is generating&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:36.960 --> 00:08:42.560
you a lot of mana. The power level point comes&nbsp;
into even sharper focus when we consider Liesa,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:42.560 --> 00:08:47.760
whose ability drains a player for 2 life whenever&nbsp;
they cast a spell. An equivalent effect is on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:47.760 --> 00:08:53.440
a fairly-costed 3 mana red enchantment called&nbsp;
Spellshock, so for two extra mana we’re slapping&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:53.440 --> 00:08:59.840
that on a 5/5 angel with a couple keywords.&nbsp;
The result is… fine. It’s okay, an adequate&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:59.840 --> 00:09:06.240
overall package, and Liesa commands a relatively&nbsp;
modest salt score of 0.8, even as a Group Slug&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:06.240 --> 00:09:11.440
commander that effectively cheats command tax.&nbsp;
One interesting exercise might be to imagine a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:11.440 --> 00:09:17.120
world in which Liesa drains opponents for 4, or&nbsp;
even 5 whenever they cast a spell instead. That&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:17.120 --> 00:09:21.680
design wouldn’t be overpowered beyond the realm&nbsp;
of possibility, but it would be pushing toward&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:21.680 --> 00:09:26.800
the upper reaches of what you might want to put&nbsp;
on a 5 mana card, and for that reason I think that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:26.800 --> 00:09:31.920
version of Liesa would be absolutely despised, a&nbsp;
monstrous commander that players shudder in fear&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:31.920 --> 00:09:36.800
thinking about. Barring counterspells, best case&nbsp;
scenario here is your opponent slams this down,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:36.800 --> 00:09:40.720
you spend 5 life and some mana to remove it, and&nbsp;
they spend some life to bring it back out again&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:40.720 --> 00:09:46.160
the following turn without any commander tax.&nbsp;
Nauseating stuff! Obviously this is more of a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:46.160 --> 00:09:51.760
hardcore group slug design, but nevertheless this&nbsp;
is the sort of power territory Yuriko lives in.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:51.760 --> 00:09:57.040
We could alternatively imagine if Yuriko had a&nbsp;
commander ninjutsu cost of 4, with everything&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:57.040 --> 00:10:02.240
else about the card remaining the same. It would&nbsp;
remain a fairly decent ninjas commander, still a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:02.240 --> 00:10:08.000
more powerful card than Liesa, but it would lose&nbsp;
its status as an unstoppable freight train of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:08.000 --> 00:10:14.000
damage and value, instead being costed similarly&nbsp;
to a Coastal Piracy, plus a creature bounce.

00:10:14.000 --> 00:10:19.040
This elucidates a weird quirk of the commander&nbsp;
format—although it’s a casual format where&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:19.040 --> 00:10:25.040
power is modulated by social factors and rule 0&nbsp;
tools such as the bracket system, power is still&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:25.040 --> 00:10:31.040
strongly felt in how upsetting a card design is. A&nbsp;
lot of designs that are unobjectionable now would&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:31.040 --> 00:10:36.720
become extremely objectionable with a single&nbsp;
tweak, a la 5 Liesa, and plenty of currently&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:36.720 --> 00:10:41.600
objectionable designs would suddenly cease to be&nbsp;
problems at all with a nerf to their power level.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:41.600 --> 00:10:46.240
This power relationship can also be seen in&nbsp;
the Salt Scores that are compiled by EDHREC&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:46.240 --> 00:10:50.080
by asking players how much they’re bothered by&nbsp;
various cards, which I’ve already referenced a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:50.080 --> 00:10:55.600
couple times in this video. The first couple dozen&nbsp;
saltiest cards all have some clear gameplay reason&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:55.600 --> 00:11:00.720
for bothering opponents, but what about Gaea’s&nbsp;
Cradle? What about Necropotence? What about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:00.720 --> 00:11:06.160
Mana Vault, or The Great Henge, or Roaming&nbsp;
Throne? These cards are purely proactive,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:06.160 --> 00:11:11.280
and none of them do anything that unique, but they&nbsp;
just so happen to be at the highest power level&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:11.280 --> 00:11:17.760
among all equivalent options, so they end up high&nbsp;
in the salt charts regardless. This relates to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:17.760 --> 00:11:23.440
fairness—even in a casual format, there might be a&nbsp;
sense of something an opponent does being unfair,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:23.440 --> 00:11:28.320
outside the bounds of ideal gameplay. I’m not&nbsp;
immune from this either—I dislike the design of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:28.320 --> 00:11:31.760
Rhystic Study in commander intrinsically&nbsp;
for a lot of reasons that I’ll probably&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:31.760 --> 00:11:36.880
get into in a future video, and I also dislike&nbsp;
playing against it. Relevant to the point here,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:36.880 --> 00:11:42.320
would I dislike Rhystic Study less if it was&nbsp;
a 5 mana card rather than a 3 mana one? Yeah,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:42.320 --> 00:11:47.120
I would. It wouldn’t solve my core gripes with&nbsp;
the card, but it would bother me less if it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:47.120 --> 00:11:52.480
cost more mana. We can kind of see how this&nbsp;
concept of fairness applies to Yuriko—a full&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:52.480 --> 00:11:57.040
command tax cheater will garner some baseline&nbsp;
level of frustration from opponents, and that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:57.040 --> 00:12:01.600
frustration will then be amplified depending&nbsp;
on the power level of the card it lives on.

00:12:03.840 --> 00:12:08.880
So, what does this all mean for the future? Should&nbsp;
Wizards of the Coast avoid cards with alternative&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:08.880 --> 00:12:14.320
command tax structures, or should they print more&nbsp;
of them? My attitude here shares some similarities&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:14.320 --> 00:12:18.480
with my attitude about Eminence cards, in the&nbsp;
sense that they’re both motivated by a sense of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:18.480 --> 00:12:24.240
curiosity and a spirit of play. I like it when&nbsp;
the mechanics of EDH format are tinkered with,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:24.240 --> 00:12:29.120
utilized, and recontextualized in ways that make&nbsp;
for interesting gameplay. I enjoy designs that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:29.120 --> 00:12:32.560
reference commanders and involve the number&nbsp;
of times a commander has been cast from the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:32.560 --> 00:12:38.000
command zone, both on commanders themselves and&nbsp;
on supporting cards. I also like alternative&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:38.000 --> 00:12:43.600
costs like the ones I’ve discussed earlier, and&nbsp;
ways of interacting with command tax in some way.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:43.600 --> 00:12:48.560
This choice of wording may hint at the line I&nbsp;
draw, though, and that’s that commanders need&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:48.560 --> 00:12:54.160
to make The Gesture: they need to acknowledge,&nbsp;
in some form or other, that they exist within&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:54.160 --> 00:12:59.120
a format where your commander costs more&nbsp;
each time it’s redeployed. Hogaak and Liesa&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:59.120 --> 00:13:04.320
both make the gesture, even if their command&nbsp;
tax feels insignificant at times. Partners&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:04.320 --> 00:13:09.520
make The Gesture—some will argue it’s unfair&nbsp;
that each partner has a separate command tax,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:09.520 --> 00:13:13.360
but how they work with command tax is&nbsp;
still an acknowledgement of the mechanic,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:13.360 --> 00:13:18.240
and is one interpretation of how it could&nbsp;
function with two commanders. On the other hand,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:18.240 --> 00:13:23.760
a big part of the reason Derevi and especially&nbsp;
Yuriko are commonly disliked commanders is that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:23.760 --> 00:13:29.680
they don’t make The Gesture. Their design totally&nbsp;
ignores the existence of command tax, and if you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:29.680 --> 00:13:35.120
play either of these you don’t even really need&nbsp;
to learn what command tax is. My reasoning for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:35.120 --> 00:13:39.600
why commanders should acknowledge the existence&nbsp;
of command tax in some form is similar to the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:39.600 --> 00:13:45.040
reasons why I think Eminence commanders should&nbsp;
feel incomplete if they go uncast—flirting with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:45.040 --> 00:13:50.000
the mechanical borders of the commander format is&nbsp;
a lovely time, but it creates a weird dynamic if&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:50.000 --> 00:13:55.360
the design you end up with feels totally outside&nbsp;
those borders. It creates an unfair feeling when&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:55.360 --> 00:13:59.680
three players are following a set of rules&nbsp;
that the other player is totally exempt from.

00:13:59.680 --> 00:14:04.720
So that’s one half of my design philosophy here,&nbsp;
and the other half is that for cards like Liesa or&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:04.720 --> 00:14:09.280
Tasigur that make the gesture but frequently&nbsp;
feel inconsequential in their casting cost,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:09.280 --> 00:14:15.120
it’s important to keep in mind how the card will&nbsp;
feel with 100% uptime. The current delve-boys,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:15.120 --> 00:14:19.440
Tasigur and Hogaak, feel fine in this way,&nbsp;
with their most powerful usages involving&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:19.440 --> 00:14:24.400
their mana value rather than what they do if they&nbsp;
stick around on board. Liesa is likewise fine,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:24.400 --> 00:14:28.720
if anything a bit modest in terms of power&nbsp;
level. The point here is just that cards that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:28.720 --> 00:14:33.120
are easier than normal to redeploy need&nbsp;
to keep that in mind with their design,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:33.120 --> 00:14:38.960
and that relates to both power level and play&nbsp;
pattern. If a commander is disgustingly powerful,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:38.960 --> 00:14:43.840
it’s going to feel especially unpleasant if&nbsp;
it’s difficult to keep off the board. Likewise,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:43.840 --> 00:14:48.560
if a card is oppressive in some way, making&nbsp;
it hard for some other decks to play the game,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:48.560 --> 00:14:53.680
the feelings of powerlessness it imbues onto those&nbsp;
decks will be amplified when conventional methods&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:53.680 --> 00:14:58.240
of dealing with the commander don’t work very&nbsp;
well. I’ve enjoyed the feeling that my opponents&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:58.240 --> 00:15:02.720
can’t escape Liesa when I’ve played with her in&nbsp;
the past, and that is a fun vibe to toy with,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:02.720 --> 00:15:08.080
but it really only works because her effect&nbsp;
puts a small tax on a plentiful resource,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:08.080 --> 00:15:13.200
and it’s up to me to try and make that plentiful&nbsp;
resource scarce. It makes it a little harder&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:13.200 --> 00:15:17.840
for me to critique Wizards of the Coast when the&nbsp;
biggest instance of a de-facto command tax cheater&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:17.840 --> 00:15:23.840
is actually a really good design, but oh well.&nbsp;
Be more like Liesa is what I’m saying, I guess.

